-- title:
--   Integrability of $\varphi(t)\cdot\Gamma_{\mathbb{R}}'/\Gamma_{\mathbb{R}}(\sigma+it)$ for quadratically decaying $\varphi$
-- statement:
--   Let $\Gamma_{\mathbb{R}}(s) = \pi^{-s/2}\Gamma(s/2)$ and let $\varphi : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{C}$ be continuous. Suppose there is a constant $C \ge 0$ with
--   $$\|\varphi(t)\| \;\le\; \frac{C}{1 + t^2} \qquad \text{for all } t \in \mathbb{R},$$
--   and let $\sigma$ satisfy $1/2 \le \sigma \le 3/2$.
--
--   Then the function
--   $$t \;\longmapsto\; \varphi(t)\,\frac{\Gamma_{\mathbb{R}}'}{\Gamma_{\mathbb{R}}}(\sigma + it)$$
--   is integrable on $\mathbb{R}$. The point is that on the strip the logarithmic derivative of $\Gamma_{\mathbb{R}}$ grows only like $\log(2 + |t|)$ (by the digamma growth bound), which multiplied by $C/(1+t^2)$ is integrable.
--
--   This generic integrability lemma serves both `integrable_Fline` (integrability of the full-line integrand) and the literal explicit formula `EF_lit_zeta`, where the Archimedean term must be a genuine Lebesgue integral.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/zeta-23-lean/blob/182afbf851aa42a8ae78507be83f2356d3a33260/Zeta23/WeilEF/FullLine.lean#L209-L270

